  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: An HPC over the Four Corners region\nresponsible for an active monsoon week over the Silver State will\ndeepen and retrograde to the west next week. This effectively \nwill cutoff monsoonal flow to the region (though CAPE/PW values \nstay robust along the eastern NV border through Wednesday) and \ncreate hazardous Heat Risk conditions across the area by end of \nthe week.\n\nFor the remainder of Saturday, radar/satellite imagery depicts\ncloud buildups and storms beginning to develop over Elko County\nthis afternoon. Storm coverage is forecast to be mainly over Elko\nCounty and northern Lander, Eureka, and White Pine Counties. PW\nvalues this afternoon over those areas are ranging 0.8-1.0 inch so\nlocalized brief heavy rainfall is possible where storms form.\nStorm motion is relatively quick but training storm motion\nespecially over locations previously receiving heavy rain this\nweek will keep minor flooding concerns in the forecast for the \nremainder of the weekend. Other hazards with storms on Saturday \ninclude brief strong wind gusts and dangerous cloud to ground \nlightning.\n\nAs the HPC deepens on Sunday monsoonal moisture is shunted \neastward and storm chances will be confined to extreme eastern\nportions of Elko and White Pine Counties along the UT border. This\nsetup will remain in place through mid week. Beyond Wednesday the\nHPC will continue to strengthen and retrograde back westward over\nthe SW CONUS. This will significantly dry out the region even \nfurther ending precipitation chances in the near future. The \nbigger concern will be the increase in afternoon high temperatures\nunder the dome of high pressure. Highs in the upper 90s and \ntriple digits late week and next weekend will increase the risk of\nheat related illness across the area. Heat advisories and other \nproducts are most certainly possible later this week as a hot \nweather pattern entrenches itself over the region.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Typical adjustments were made to\nthe Wind/WindGust grids as NBM normally undervalues wind speeds\nfor hot/dry/windy conditions such as those created in NW NV today.\nHigh confidence exists in shower and storm chances over eastern NV\nSaturday afternoon. A combination of SST and SerpISC was used to\nedit hourly Wx grids this afternoon as NBM misrepresented storm\nchances and coverage today. Moderate confidence exists in\nlingering storms over the eastern border through Wednesday.\nModerate confidence exists in above average temperatures and\nHeatRisk later this week and next weekend.",
